A||||4||Jncomutt||Luggage Rack Removal||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||08-07-2001||09:58 AM||||I just ordered my wrap around spoiler for my 84.  I need to now take off the luggage rack thats there.  How do I get the darn thing off?  Also, any suggestions or tips to help me keep the removal as 'clean' as possible?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||08-07-2001||10:06 AM||||Take off the strips from the tops of the rails and remove the screws.  Shouldn't be too hard.  Are you going to glass the holes in?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jncomutt||08-07-2001||10:08 AM||||Thanks.  Yes, I'm going to cover them up, not sure if I'm going to glass them, I've never used that before.  Or just bondo.  I'm planning to have the whole lid repainted after the install.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||08-07-2001||10:10 AM||||I am going to assume that the luggage rack on your 84 is secured in the same way as the ones on my 85SE and 85GT.<P>If you look closely at the luggage rack strips you'll see that there is a plastic insert in the center of each strip running from end to end.  Use a small screw driver or awl and pry this strip up on one end.  Once you get it started it will pull out without damaging anything.  Now you can see that there are two (maybe three on some) screws holding the parts down to the lid.  The outer parts (where the cross member meets the side rails) also have hex head nuts to be removed from the under side of the deck lid.<P>With the screws removed and the luggage rack pulled off you'll see rubber inserts pressed into holes in the lid.  These expand as the screws are tightened down and can be pulled out of the deck lid holes.  This will leave several holes about 5/8inch in diameter in the deck lid.  If left open the deck lid will allow rain water to enter the trunk.<P>You can either fiberglass the holes in and repaint the lid or get a lid off of a donor car that does not have the holes.<P>Usually the paint under where the luggage rack parts were mounted will not exactly match the rest of the lid's paint because it has not been exposed to the elements for 17 years.  You can try buffing it out but you will still need to fill in the holes anyway.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jncomutt||08-07-2001||02:03 PM||||Got it off no problem.  I didn't realize that the center piece was riveted to the back and cracked it.  But other than that, all else was good.  <P>Now to fill in the holes...   ||||reg||1
